, son of Ragnar Lothbrok, led a fleet intent on conquering Rome. His tutor, the raider Hastein, planned to crown him emperor there. A Viking fleet really did sail, past Spain, into the Mediterranean, then struck southern France, and finally raided Italy. At a walled city by the sea, Hastein believed he had found Rome. But the gates were barred. So Hastein sent word that he was dying, and wished to become a Christian. The bishop and count could have kept the gates shut. They chose mercy, and Hastein was baptised. Then, on Hastein's orders, his companions staged their grief. They said he was dead, and wished to be buried in the city. The bishop let the funeral in. Behind the coffin, the mourners wore armour under their tunics. Mid-funeral, Hastein leapt from the coffin and struck the bishop down. Only after the city fell did the raiders learn the truth. This wasn't Rome. It was Luna, an old Roman town. But the strangest mistake may be the name. The fleet was real, but the Frankish annals named no captain. The oldest surviving funeral story, by the churchman Dudo, gave Hastein the sword, and never mentioned Bj\u00f6rn. Decades later, a monk named William placed Bj\u00f6rn aboard. They conquered the wrong Rome. Perhaps history crowned the wrong Viking. <break time=\"0.8s\"/> History remembers the victors. We remember the rest.", "src_md5": "ff97caaa20d2238fddc30c7bdb4a0c8a", "voice": "jfIS2w2yJi0grJZPyEsk", "model": "eleven_multilingual_v2", "voice_settings": {"stability": 0.5, "similarity_boost": 0.75, "style": 0.0, "use_speaker_boost": true}, "seed": 1611}